on of FIPS support posture and prerequisites | P2 | E008 |\n\nAll four HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements; no HLR split across\nredundant epics.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Platform Operator not in architecture context** (non-blocking, verify):\n   `.context/architecture-context/` contains only the KServe file. Per the\n   operational platform-inclusion check the Platform Operator is treated as\n   not-yet-onboarded and an onboarding chain (E001–E003) was generated. If\n   the operator already exists in the platform (i.e., the context gap is a\n   data gap, not a real gap), E001–E003 should be dropped and E004 re-based\n   onto the existing repo/pipeline.\n2. **Team attribution assumed** (non-blocking): the strategy contains no team\n   data. RHOAI Serving comes from the KServe architecture context; \"RHOAI\n   Platform\" for the Platform Operator epics is an assumption (no\n   architecture context available); the docs epic is assigned to RHOAI\n   Serving as subject-matter owner.\n3. **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams, Dependencies table,\n   Acceptance Criteria, and NFRs are absent; acceptance criteria were derived\n   from HLRs and the problem statement.\n4. **External schedule constraints are not ACs**: lab turnaround (historically\n   six to nine months) and review-board cadence are externally controlled.\n   They are recorded as schedule risk on E007; E007's ACs are written at the\n   engineering completion point (package assembled, review-board sign-off\n   recorded, package submitted to the lab and triaged).\n5. **Docs-epic priority**: HLR-4 is P2, but per the `docs-authoring` priority\n   exception the docs epic derives P0 from the strategy's Critical priority.\n   This does not push priority upstream (its P0 prerequisites are already P0).\n6. **CI gate enablement sequencing**: E006 is parallel to E005 by DAG rules\n   (independent work products). Operationally the gate should be enabled once\n   the remediated baseline exists — captured as