{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-1c9eb01f-2c76-4892-8773-d77acdf29b81","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-96a448c5e67bf039","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9013-lv3l_5nh/artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-9013-decomp-review.md","content":"# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9013\n\n## Review Summary\n\nScore: 11/14 — fail\nRecommendation: revise\n\nContract validator: OK (body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11\ndocs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass).\n\nTriage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with 8 epics —\nmulti-epic output is expected; no contradiction.\n\nThe decomposition is strong on most dimensions — HLR coverage, boundaries,\ntyping, ACs, and completeness are all clean, and it handles the classic traps\ncorrectly (external lab/review-board schedule recorded as risk on E007 rather\nthan as ACs; E003∥E004 kept parallel with the Rule 25 AC carrying the\ncoupling; docs-authoring priority derived from the strategy's Critical\npriority). It fails on one structural defect: a missing Rule 6 DAG edge\n(E001 → E004), which zeroes DAG Coherence and triggers the auto-fail rule.\n\n## Criterion Details\n\n### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n\nAll four HLRs map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix in the\nsummary is complete and consistent with the epic files:\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): E005 (KServe + serving data plane) and E004 (Platform\n  Operator), enabled by the onboarding chain E001–E003.\n- HLR-2 (P0): E007, with inputs correctly traced to E003 (attestations),\n  E004/E005 (inventory, test results).\n- HLR-3 (P1): E006.\n- HLR-4 (P2): E008.\n\nPriority inheritance is correct: E001/E002/E003 are P0 and sit in the\ndependency closure of the P0 work they enable (E004, E007). E006 (P1) blocks\nonly E008, a `docs-authoring` epic exempt from priority inheritance — not a\nviolation. E008's P0 correctly derives from the docs-authoring exception\n(strategy priority Critical → P0), not from inheritance, and the summary says\nso explicitly. No priority collapse: each epic maps to HLRs at a single\npriority level; no deferrable lower-priority feature is bundled into a P0\nepic.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n\n**Critical — missing edge E001 → E004 (Rule 6).** Rule 6 mandates\n`repo-onboarding` → general implementation of the onboarded component always\nserial (\"code needs repo\"). E004 is the general implementation of the Platform\nOperator, the component E001 onboards, and E004's scope explicitly states it\n\"ship[s] the changes through the operator's midstream repository ... provisioned\nfor this component\" — yet E004's frontmatter `dependencies` lists only\nRHAISTRAT-9013-E002. The edge is absent from the summary DAG diagram and from\nthe DAG justification table (which covers E001→E003, E001∥E002, E002→E004,\nE003∥E004, but never E001↔E004). The repo is a genuine artifact E004 consumes,\ndiscoverable directly from E004's scope, so this is a missing data/artifact\ndependency edge — critical. Note the asymmetry in the rules is deliberate: the\nKonflux pipeline coupling is AC-gated (Rule 10/25), but the repository coupling\nhas no such carve-out — code cannot be developed in a repo that does not exist.\nFix: add RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 to E004's `dependencies` and to the diagram and\njustification table.\n\nEverything else in the DAG is correct:\n\n- All 12 diagram edges match epic frontmatter `dependencies` in both\n  directions; arrow direction (dependency → dependent) is correct; no\n  circular dependencies; no edges to nonexistent epics; no branch files.\n- Rules 7/8/10 pairings verified against every general-implementation epic:\n  E001∥E002 (no edge), E002∥E003 (no edge), and E003∥E004 (no edge, with the\n  Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC present on E004). E003 has no edge to\n  E005 or E006.\n- The E003 → E007 edge was examined against Rule 10 and accepted: E007 is not\n  the Platform Operator's component implementation (it is the cross-stack\n  evidence-package epic owned by RHOAI Serving), and the consumed item —\n  signed build attestations — is a genuine deliverable artifact named in\n  HLR-2 and mandated by E003's own AC, not an image digest/SHA/URL\n  configuration reference. Rule 13 licenses this edge, and the completeness\n  cross-check would require an edge for exactly this consumption. It also\n  adds no serialization beyond the existing E004→E007 path (E004's Rule 25 AC\n  already forces the pipeline green before E004 completes).\n- Critical path length 4 (E001→E003→E007→E008) is within the M-with-new-\n  component heuristic (4–5) and matches the summary frontmatter.\n\nMinor: E002's `existing_foundation` rationale (\"a source tree exists to scan\nonce the repository is provisioned\") and its scope (\"over the Platform\nOperator repository\") imply scanning the midstream repo E001 creates,\nundercutting the declared Rule 7 parallelism. Since no edge may exist, the\nscope/rationale should say the scan runs against the upstream source tree.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: E001–E004 (Platform Operator /\nRHOAI Platform), E005–E007 (KServe / RHOAI Serving), E008 (Documentation /\nRHOAI Serving). No epic bundles multiple teams. No scope duplication — E005's\nmodule inventory is produced once and consumed by E007; E004's audit covers\nonly operator images. Code implementation and documentation authoring are\nseparated (E008 vs. the rest). Epic sizes are plausible single-sprint units;\nhealth warning 7 correctly flags the one size risk (data-plane span) for\nverification. Team attribution assumptions are disclosed in health warnings.\n\nMinor: E007 is assigned component `KServe` although its scope spans the whole\nserving stack including Platform Operator images. The summary discloses and\njustifies this (most-relevant canonical name), and it is one logical change\n(evidence assembly), so it is not a boundary violation — but the span should\nbe kept visible if the operator work is re-based (see health warning 1).\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nNo Investigation epics — correctly justified: the strategy's Open Questions\nsection is empty on scope; lab/review-board timing is an external schedule\nconstraint recorded as risk on E007, not a technical unknown that changes\nwhich epics exist; the Platform Operator architecture-context gap correctly\ntriggers the Rule 20 onboarding chain (it is a component, not infrastructure,\nso Rule 22 does not apply), with the data-gap risk disclosed as health\nwarning 1. Every epic produces a concrete artifact. No `gated_by` or\n`gate_failure_impact` fields exist, and none are needed (no Investigation\ndependencies anywhere).\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n\nAll eight epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals`\n(none uses `investigation_signals`); all eight ai-signals rationale files\nexist, match the frontmatter values, and are substantive. Computed\n`ai_implementability`/`ai_implementability_score` fields are consistent with\nthe written signals (not re-derived per instructions). Most signal values are\nwell justified — notably E007's -1 on `external_dependency` and\n`human_process_gates` (accredited lab, mandatory review board) and E004's -1\non `architecture_claims` (no architecture context for the Platform Operator).\n\nHowever, the rubric is applied inconsistently across sibling epics for the\nsame conditions:\n\n- `human_process_gates`: E005 and E006 score 1 with the rationale \"standard\n  PR review only; the security review board gates the certification\n  submission, not this code change\", while E001–E004 and E008 score 0 with\n  materially identical rationales (\"standard org conventions; no approval\n  board beyond normal review\", \"standard PR review\"). The rubric's +1\n  condition is \"None\" — same condition, different values.\n- `external_dependency`: E001–E003 score 0 although each rationale states the\n  +1 condition (\"no upstream contribution or vendor coordination needed\",\n  \"scanning is internal\", \"Konflux is internal platform infrastructure\"),\n  while E004, E006, and E008 score 1 for the same \"none needed\" condition.\n- E004's `open_questions: 0` is borderline: the rationale concedes \"the\n  change surface itself is undefined\", which is the rubric's -1 condition\n  (an open question that changes implementation approach); parking it under\n  `change_specificity: -1` is defensible but arguable.\n\nThese are conservative-direction scoring inconsistencies (they deflate, not\ninflate, implementability), so the criterion is not zeroed, but they should\nbe normalized during revision.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are\npresent where applicable: E008 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review AC\n(Rule 24); E004 has the \"build pipeline green\" AC (Rule 25) as does E005 for\nits existing pipelines; E006 has the deliberately-non-compliant fixture AC.\nRule 23 (rollback/feature-flag) was considered and judged not applicable —\nthis is crypto-module remediation within images, not replacement of an\nexisting capability with a new implementation. The canonical soft-AC trap is\nhandled correctly: lab turnaround and review-board cadence live in E007's\nRisks/Notes and the summary's Health Warnings, and E007's ACs are written at\nthe engineering completion point (assembled, sign-off recorded, submitted and\ntriaged). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is covered: image remediation (E004/E005), evidence\npackage and submission (E007), CI enforcement (E006), documentation (E008),\nonboarding for the component missing from architecture context (E001–E003).\nThe out-of-scope boundaries (workbenches/pipelines/training, Common Criteria)\nare carried