{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-85cae8ab4b9eedae","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing DAG edge E001 -> E004: Rule 6 mandates repo-onboarding ->\n10\t    general implementation of the onboarded component always serial (code needs repo).\n11\t    E004 (Platform Operator FIPS implementation) ships through the midstream repository\n12\t    provisioned by E001, but its dependencies list only E002; the edge is absent from\n13\t    the summary DAG diagram and justification table. Add RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 to E004\n14\t    dependencies and to the diagram.'\n15\t- severity: minor\n16\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n17\t  description: E002 scope and existing_foundation rationale imply scanning the midstream\n18\t    repository created by E001 ('once the repository is provisioned'), contradicting\n19\t    the declared Rule 7 parallelism; the scan target should be stated as the upstream\n20\t    source tree.\n21\t- severity: minor\n22\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n23\t  description: 'human_process_gates scored inconsistently: E005/E006 score 1 for ''standard\n24\t    PR review only'' while E001-E004 and E008 score 0 with materially identical rationales;\n25\t    the rubric +1 condition (no human approval step) holds for all of them.'\n26\t- severity: minor\n27\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n28\t  description: external_dependency scored 0 on E001-E003 although each rationale states\n29\t    the +1 condition (no upstream contribution or vendor coordination needed), inconsistent\n30\t    with E004/E006/E008 which score 1 for the same condition.\n31\t- severity: minor\n32\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n33\t  description: E007 is assigned component KServe although its scope spans the whole\n34\t    serving stack including Platform Operator images (disclosed in the summary as\n35\t    most-relevant canonical name); keep the span visible if the operator work is re-based\n36\t    per health warning 1.\n37\terror: null\n38\t---\n39\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9013\n40\t\n41\t## Review Summary\n42\t\n43\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n44\tRecommendation: revise\n45\t\n46\tContract validator: OK (body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11\n47\tdocs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass).\n48\t\n49\tTriage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with 8 epics —\n50\tmulti-epic output is expected; no contradiction.\n51\t\n52\tThe decomposition is strong on most dimensions — HLR coverage, boundaries,\n53\ttyping, ACs, and completeness are all clean, and it handles the classic traps\n54\tcorrectly (external lab/review-board schedule recorded as risk on E007 rather\n55\tthan as ACs; E003∥E004 kept parallel with the Rule 25 AC carrying the\n56\tcoupling; docs-authoring priority derived from the strategy's Critical\n57\tpriority). It fails on one structural defect: a missing Rule 6 DAG edge\n58\t(E001 → E004), which zeroes DAG Coherence and triggers the auto-fail rule.\n59\t\n60\t## Criterion Details\n61\t\n62\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n63\t\n64\tAll four HLRs map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix in the\n65\tsummary is complete and consistent with the epic files:\n66\t\n67\t- HLR-1 (P0): E005 (KServe + serving data plane) and E004 (Platform\n68\t  Operator), enabled by the onboarding chain E001–E003.\n69\t- HLR-2 (P0): E007, with inputs correctly traced to E003 (attestations),\n70\t  E004/E005 (inventory, test results).\n71\t- HLR-3 (P1): E006.\n72\t- HLR-4 (P2): E008.\n73\t\n74\tPriority inheritance is correct: E001/E002/E003 are P0 and sit in the\n75\tdependency closure of the P0 work they enable (E004, E007). E006 (P1) blocks\n76\tonly E008, a `docs-authoring` epic exempt from priority inheritance — not a\n77\tviolation. E008's P0 correctly derives from the docs-authoring exception\n78\t(strategy priority Critical → P0), not from inheritance, and the summary says\n79\tso explicitly. No priority collapse: each epic maps to HLRs at a single\n80\tpriority level; no deferrable lower-priority feature is bundled into a P0\n81\tepic.\n82\t\n83\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n84\t\n85\t**Critical — missing edge E001 → E004 (Rule 6).** Rule 6 mandates\n86\t`repo-onboarding` → general implementation of the onboarded component always\n87\tserial (\"code needs repo\"). E004 is the general implementation of the Platform\n88\tOperator, the component E001 onboards, and E004's scope explicitly states it\n89\t\"ship[s] the changes through the operator's midstream repository ... provisioned\n90\tfor this component\" — yet E004's frontmatter `dependencies` lists only\n91\tRHAISTRAT-9013-E002. The edge is absent from the summary DAG diagram and from\n92\tthe DAG justification table (which covers E001→E003, E001∥E002, E002→E004,\n93\tE003∥E004, but never E001↔E004). The repo is a genuine artifact E004 consumes,\n94\tdiscoverable directly from E004's scope, so this is a missing data/artifact\n95\tdependency edge — critical. Note the asymmetry in the rules is deliberate: the\n96\tKonflux pipeline coupling is AC-gated (Rule 10/25), but the repository coupling\n97\thas no such carve-out — code cannot be developed in a repo that does not exist.\n98\tFix: add RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 to E004's `dependencies` and to the diagram and\n99\tjustification table.\n100\t\n101\tEverything else in the DAG is correct:\n102\t\n103\t- All 12 diagram edges match epic frontmatter `dependencies` in both\n104\t  directions; arrow direction (dependency → dependent) is correct; no\n105\t  circular dependencies; no edges to nonexistent epics; no branch files.\n106\t- Rules 7/8/10 pairings verified against every general-implementation epic:\n107\t  E001∥E002 (no edge), E002∥E003 (no edge), and E003∥E004 (no edge, with the\n108\t  Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC present on E004). E003 has no edge to\n109\t  E005 or E006.\n110\t- The E003 → E007 edge was examined against Rule 10 and accepted: E007 is not\n111\t  the Platform Operator's component implementation (it is the cross-stack\n112\t  evidence-package epic owned by RHOAI Serving), and the consumed item —\n113\t  signed build attestations — is a genuine deliverable artifact named in\n114\t  HLR-2 and mandated by E003's own AC, not an image digest/SHA/URL\n115\t  configuration reference. Rule 13 licenses this edge, and the completeness\n116\t  cross-check would require an edge for exactly this consumption. It also\n117\t  adds no serialization beyond the existing E004→E007 path (E004's Rule 25 AC\n118\t  already forces the pipeline green before E004 completes).\n119\t- Critical path length 4 (E001→E003→E007→E008) is within the M-with-new-\n120\t  component heuristic (4–5) and matches the summary frontmatter.\n121\t\n122\tMinor: E002's `existing_foundation` rationale (\"a source tree exists to scan\n123\tonce the repository is provisioned\") and its scope (\"over the Platform\n124\tOperator repository\") imply scanning the midstream repo E001 creates,\n125\tundercutting the declared Rule 7 parallelism. Since no edge may exist, the\n126\tscope/rationale should say the scan runs against the upstream source tree.\n127\t\n128\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n129\t\n130\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: E001–E004 (Platform Operator /\n131\tRHOAI Platform), E005–E007 (KServe / RHOAI Serving), E008 (Documentation /\n132\tRHOAI Serving). No epic bundles multiple teams. No scope duplication — E005's\n133\tmodule inventory is produced once and consumed by E007; E004's audit covers\n134\tonly operator images. Code implementation and documentation authoring are\n135\tseparated (E008 vs. the rest). Epic sizes are plausible single-sprint units;\n136\thealth warning 7 correctly flags the one size risk (data-plane span) for\n137\tverification. Team attribution assumptions are disclosed in health warnings.\n138\t\n139\tMinor: E007 is assigned component `KServe` although its scope spans the whole\n140\tserving stack including Platform Operator images. The summary discloses and\n141\tjustifies this (most-relevant canonical name), and it is one logical change\n142\t(evidence assembly), so it is not a boundary violation — but the span should\n143\tbe kept visible if the operator work is re-based (see health warning 1).\n144\t\n145\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n146\t\n147\tNo Investigation epics — correctly justified: the strategy's Open Questions\n148\tsection is empty on scope; lab/review-board timing is an external schedule\n149\tconstraint recorded as risk on E007, not a technical unknown that changes\n150\twhich epics exist; the Platform Operator architecture-context gap correctly\n151\ttriggers the Rule 20 onboarding chain (it is a component, not infrastructure,\n152\tso Rule 22 does not apply), with the data-gap risk disclosed as health\n153\twarning 1. Every epic produces a concrete artifact. No `gated_by` or\n154\t`gate_failure_impact` fields exist, and none are needed (no Investigation\n155\tdependencies anywhere).\n156\t\n157\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n158\t\n159\tAll eight epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals`\n160\t(none uses `investigation_signals`); all eight ai-signals rationale files\n161\texist, match the frontmatter values, and are substantive. Computed\n162\t`ai_implementability`/`ai_implementability_score` fields are consistent with\n163\tthe written signals (not re-derived per instructions). Most signal values are\n164\twell justified — notably E007's -1 on `external_dependency` and\n165\t`human_process_gates` (accredited lab, mandatory review board) and E004's -1\n166\ton `architecture_claims` (no architecture context for the Platform Operator).\n167\t\n168\tHowever, the rubric is applied inconsistently across sibling epics for the\n169\tsame conditions:\n170\t\n171\t- `human_process_gates