{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d8f255f896f1dcf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013\n10\t\n11\tFIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack.\n12\t\n13\t## Triage Outcome\n14\t\n15\tNot below-threshold and not docs-only — full multi-epic decomposition:\n16\t\n17\t- The strategy spans two components (KServe, Platform Operator) and multiple\n18\t  architecturally distinct work streams (image crypto remediation, CI\n19\t  enforcement, compliance evidence assembly with external submission,\n20\t  documentation authoring).\n21\t- HLRs span P0–P2 and the certification path involves external human gates.\n22\t\n23\t## Step 1 — Parsed Strategy Structure\n24\t\n25\t- **Affected Components**: KServe, Platform Operator (plain list; no owner\n26\t  teams given).\n27\t- **HLRs**: HLR-1 (P0, FIPS-correct serving-stack images), HLR-2 (P0,\n28\t  compliance evidence package submitted through security review board to the\n29\t  external certification lab), HLR-3 (P1, CI gating check for FIPS\n30\t  regressions), HLR-4 (P2, documentation of FIPS support posture and\n31\t  prerequisites).\n32\t- **Staff Engineer Input**: absent — no overrides to log.\n33\t- **Missing sections** (health warnings, decomposition proceeded from HLRs and\n34\t  the problem statement): Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n35\t  Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), Non-Functional Requirements.\n36\t\n37\t## Component Graph\n38\t\n39\t- **KServe** — active component. Architecture context exists\n40\t  (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`): owner **RHOAI Serving**, midstream\n41\t  `opendatahub-io/kserve`, already Konflux-onboarded. Generates the\n42\t  remediation, CI, evidence, and documentation epics.\n43\t- **Platform Operator** — active component. **No architecture context file\n44\t  exists**, so per the platform-inclusion check it is treated as a component\n45\t  not yet in the platform: an onboarding chain (repo-onboarding +\n46\t  license-validation → konflux-onboarding → general implementation) is\n47\t  generated for it (Rule 20). See Health Warnings — this is flagged for human\n48\t  verification because the strategy does not narrate the Platform Operator as\n49\t  new.\n50\t- No passive components (both affected components require changes).\n51\t- **Component name mappings** (canonical names from\n52\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`): the crypto remediation epic covers \"KServe\n53\t  components and the serving data plane\" — assigned canonical component\n54\t  `KServe` (span noted). The evidence-package epic spans the whole serving\n55\t  stack including operator images — assigned `KServe` as the most-relevant\n56\t  canonical name. The documentation epic is assigned `Documentation`.\n57\t\n58\t## Investigation Epics\n59\t\n60\tNone. The strategy's Open Questions section states \"None on scope\". The\n61\tremaining uncertainty — lab scheduling and review-board cadence — is an\n62\texternal schedule constraint, not a technical unknown that changes which epics\n63\texist: it is captured as schedule risk on the evidence-package epic, not as an\n64\tInvestigation. The Platform Operator architecture-context gap is handled by\n65\tthe onboarding chain plus a health warning (no dependency-availability\n66\tunknown to resolve). The specific images linking non-validated crypto are\n67\tenumerated by the audit inside the remediation epic (implementation detail,\n68\tAC-gated), which also produces the module inventory HLR-2 requires.\n69\t\n70\t## Epic List\n71\t\n72\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n73\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n74\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | Onboard Platform Operator midstream repository | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n75\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | License