h `repo-onboarding`: independent inputs. |\n111\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 9 | `license-validation` → general implementation serial: license failures would change dependencies and approach. |\n112\t| E003 ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rule 10 | `konflux-onboarding` parallel with the general implementation — the \"build pipeline green\" AC on E004 enforces the coupling; a DAG edge would serialize work that must run in parallel. |\n113\t| E005 (no deps) | Rule 14 | Different repo from all Platform Operator work; KServe is already in-platform and Konflux-onboarded. |\n114\t| E006 (no deps) | Rule 15 | Same repo as E005 but a different area (CI gating config vs. image contents) — coordination, not dependency. The coupling to the remediated baseline is an AC on E006 (check passes on remediated images), not a DAG edge. |\n115\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 13 | The evidence package includes build attestations produced by the Platform Operator's Konflux pipeline — an artifact the package consumes; it cannot be assembled without them. |\n116\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 13 | The evidence package documents the operator's remediated images (module inventory, test results). |\n117\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 13 | The evidence package documents the KServe/data-plane remediated images and includes the module inventory E005 produces. |\n118\t| E001–E007 → E008 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` blocked by ALL Implementation epics in the strategy; docs describe what was built. |\n119\t\n120\tPriority inheritance: E001, E002, E003 are P0 because they sit in the\n121\tdependency closure of the P0 evidence-package epic (E007) and enable the P0\n122\tPlatform Operator implementation (E004). E008's P0 comes from the\n123\t`docs-authoring` priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority Critical →\n124\tP0), not from inheritance — see Health Warnings.\n125\t\n126\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n127\t\n128\t| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n129\t|-----|----------|-------|\n130\t| HLR-1 — serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto | P0 | E005 (KServe + serving data plane), E004 (Platform Operator); enabled by onboarding chain E001, E002, E003 |\n131\t| HLR-2 — compliance evidence package assembled and submitted through security review board to external lab | P0 | E007 (attestations sourced from E003's pipeline, inventory and test results from E004/E005) |\n132\t| HLR-3 — CI enforces FIPS regressions via gating check | P1 | E006 |\n133\t| HLR-4 — documentation of FIPS support posture and prerequisites | P2 | E008 |\n134\t\n135\tAll four HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements; no HLR split across\n136\tredundant epics.\n137\t\n138\t## Health Warnings\n139\t\n140\t1. **Platform Operator not in architecture context** (non-blocking, verify):\n141\t   `.context/architecture-context/` contains only the KServe file. Per the\n142\t   operational platform-inclusion check the Platform Operator is treated as\n143\t   not-yet-onboarded and an onboarding chain (E001–E003) was generated. If\n144\t   the operator already exists in the platform (i.e., the context gap is a\n145\t   data gap, not a real gap), E001–E003 should be dropped and E004 re-based\n146\t   onto the existing repo/pipeline.\n147\t2. **Team attribution assumed** (non-blocking): the strategy contains no team\n148\t   data. RHOAI Serving comes from the KServe architecture context; \"RHOAI\n149\t   Platform\" for the Platform Operator epics is an assumption (no\n150\t   architecture context available); the docs epic is assigned to RHOAI\n151\t   Serving as subject-matter owner.\n152\t3. **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams, Dependencies table,\n153\t   Acceptance Criteria, and NFRs are absent; acceptance criteria were derived\n154\t   from HLRs and the problem statement.\n155\t4. **External schedule constraints are not ACs**: lab turnaround (historically\n156\t   six to nine months) and review-board cadence are externally controlled.\n157\t   They are recorded as schedule risk on E007; E007's ACs are written at the\n158\t   engineering completion point (package assembled, review-board sign-off\n159\t   recorded, package submitted to the lab and triaged).\n160\t5. **Docs-epic priority**: HLR-4 is P2, but per the `docs-authoring` priority\n161\t   exception the docs epic derives P0 from the strategy's Critical priority.\n162\t   This does not push priority upstream (its P0 prerequisites are already P0).\n163\t6. **CI gate enablement sequencing**: E006 is parallel to E005 by DAG rules\n164\t   (independent work products). Operationally the gate should be enabled once\n165\t   the remediated baseline exists — captured as an AC on E006, and as a\n166\t   coordination note here.\n167\t7. **Serving data plane span**: the problem statement includes the serving\n168\t   data plane (runtime images) in the crypto remediation, while Affected\n169\t   Components lists only KServe and Platform Operator. The remediation epic\n170\t   covers serving-stack images including the data plane under canonical\n171\t   component `KServe`; if data-plane runtime remediation turns out to need\n172\t   separate repos/review cycles, it may warrant its own epic under\n173\t   `Serving Runtimes`.\n174\t\n175\t## Tiered Delivery\n176\t\n177\tThe external certification lab has uncertain, externally controlled timing,\n178\tbut the strategy delivers value without the certification outcome:\n179\t\n180\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001–E006 — FIPS-correct serving-stack\n181\t  images running on FIPS-enabled clusters, CI regression protection, and the\n182\t  onboarded operator repo/pipeline. None of this is gated by the lab.\n183\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: E007 — evidence-package submission and the\n184\t  certification outcome. The submission milestone is engineered (package\n185\t  assembled, review-board sign-off, submitted and triaged); the lab's\n186\t  certification decision and turnaround are not compressible by engineering\n187\t  effort.\n188\t\n189\tNo upstream PR/RFC epic is needed (r