{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bed413926210da9a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013\n11\t\n12\tFIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Outcome\n15\t\n16\tNot below-threshold and not docs-only — full multi-epic decomposition:\n17\t\n18\t- The strategy spans two components (KServe, Platform Operator) and multiple\n19\t  architecturally distinct work streams (image crypto remediation, CI\n20\t  enforcement, compliance evidence assembly with external submission,\n21\t  documentation authoring).\n22\t- HLRs span P0–P2 and the certification path involves external human gates.\n23\t\n24\t## Step 1 — Parsed Strategy Structure\n25\t\n26\t- **Affected Components**: KServe, Platform Operator (plain list; no owner\n27\t  teams given).\n28\t- **HLRs**: HLR-1 (P0, FIPS-correct serving-stack images), HLR-2 (P0,\n29\t  compliance evidence package submitted through security review board to the\n30\t  external certification lab), HLR-3 (P1, CI gating check for FIPS\n31\t  regressions), HLR-4 (P2, documentation of FIPS support posture and\n32\t  prerequisites).\n33\t- **Staff Engineer Input**: absent — no overrides to log.\n34\t- **Missing sections** (health warnings, decomposition proceeded from HLRs and\n35\t  the problem statement): Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n36\t  Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), Non-Functional Requirements.\n37\t\n38\t## Component Graph\n39\t\n40\t- **KServe** — active component. Architecture context exists\n41\t  (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`): owner **RHOAI Serving**, midstream\n42\t  `opendatahub-io/kserve`, already Konflux-onboarded. Generates the\n43\t  remediation, CI, evidence, and documentation epics.\n44\t- **Platform Operator** — active component. **No architecture context file\n45\t  exists**, so per the platform-inclusion check it is treated as a component\n46\t  not yet in the platform: an onboarding chain (repo-onboarding +\n47\t  license-validation → konflux-onboarding → general implementation) is\n48\t  generated for it (Rule 20). See Health Warnings — this is flagged for human\n49\t  verification because the strategy does not narrate the Platform Operator as\n50\t  new.\n51\t- No passive components (both affected components require changes).\n52\t- **Component name mappings** (canonical names from\n53\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`): the crypto remediation epic covers \"KServe\n54\t  components and the serving data plane\" — assigned canonical component\n55\t  `KServe` (span noted). The evidence-package epic spans the whole serving\n56\t  stack including operator images — assigned `KServe` as the most-relevant\n57\t  canonical name. The documentation epic is assigned `Documentation`.\n58\t\n59\t## Investigation Epics\n60\t\n61\tNone. The strategy's Open Questions section states \"None on scope\". The\n62\tremaining uncertainty — lab scheduling and review-board cadence — is an\n63\texternal schedule constraint, not a technical unknown that changes which epics\n64\texist: it is captured as schedule risk on the evidence-package epic, not as an\n65\tInvestigation. The Platform Operator architecture-context gap is handled by\n66\tthe onboarding chain plus a health warning (no dependency-availability\n67\tunknown to resolve). The specific images linking non-validated crypto are\n68\tenumerated by the audit inside the remediation epic (implementation detail,\n69\tAC-gated), which also produces the module inventory HLR-2 requires.\n70\t\n71\t## Epic List\n72\t\n73\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n74\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n75\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | Onboard Platform Operator midstream repository | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n76\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | License validation for Platform Operator dependencies | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n77\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | Konflux onboarding for Platform Operator build pipeline | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n78\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | FIPS 140-3 compliance for Platform Operator images | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n79\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | FIPS 140-3 remediation of KServe and serving data plane images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n80\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 | CI gating check for FIPS crypto regressions in serving-stack builds | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n81\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E007 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n82\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E008 | Document FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n83\t\n84\tImplementation types: E001 `repo-onboarding`, E002 `license-validation`,\n85\tE003 `konflux-onboarding`, E008 `docs-authoring`; E004–E007 general\n86\timplementations.\n87\t\n88\t## Dependency DAG\n89\t\n90\t```mermaid\n91\tgraph TD\n92\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 repo-onboarding] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 konflux-onboarding]\n93\t    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 operator FIPS impl]\n94\t    E001 --> E008[RHAISTRAT-9013-E008 docs]\n95\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 license-validation] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 operator FIPS impl]\n96\t    E002 --> E008\n97\t    E003 --> E007[RHAISTRAT-9013-E007 evidence package]\n98\t    E003 --> E008\n99\t    E004 --> E007\n100\t    E004 --> E008\n101\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 KServe FIPS remediation] --> E007\n102\t    E005 --> E008\n103\t    E006[RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 CI FIPS gate] --> E008\n104\t    E007 --> E008\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\t## DAG Justification\n108\t\n109\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n110\t|------|------|-----------|\n111\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 5 | `repo-onboarding` → `konflux-onboarding` always serial: the pipeline needs the repo. |\n112\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 6 | `repo-onboarding` → general implementation of the onboarded component always serial: E004's FIPS changes are developed and shipped through the midstream repository E001 provisions — code needs repo. |\n113\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 7 | `license-validation` parallel with `repo-onboarding`: independent inputs — the license scan runs against the upstream Platform Operator source tree, not the midstream repository. |\n114\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 9 | `license-validation` → general implementation serial: license failures would change dependencies and approach. |\n115\t| E003 ∥ E004 (no edge) | Rule 10 | `konflux-onboarding` parallel with the general implementation — the \"build pipeline green\" AC on E004 enforces the coupling; a DAG edge would serialize work that must run in parallel. |\n116\t| E005 (no deps) | Rule 14 | Different repo from all Platform Operator work; KServe is already in-platform and Konflux-onboarded. |\n117\t| E006 (no deps) | Rule 15 | Same repo as E005 but a different area (CI gating config vs. image contents) — coordination, not dependency. The coupling to the remediated baseline is an AC on E006 (check passes on remediated images), not a DAG edge. |\n118\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 13 | The evidence package includes build attestations produced by the Platform Operator's Konflux pipeline — an artifact the package consumes; it cannot be assembled without them. |\n119\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 13 | The evidence package documents the operator's remediated images (module inventory, test results). |\n120\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 13 | The evidence package documents the KServe/data-plane remediated images and includes the module inventory E005 produces. |\n121\t| E001–E007 → E008 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` blocked by ALL Implementation epics in the strategy; docs describe what was built. |\n122\t\n123\tPriority inheritance: E001, E002, E003 are P0 because they sit in the\n124\tdependency closure of the P0 evidence-package epic (E007) and enable the P0\n125\tPlatform Operator implementation (E004). E008's P0 comes from the\n126\t`docs-authoring` priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority Critical →\n127\tP0), not from inheritance — see Health Warnings.\n128\t\n129\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n130\t\n131\t| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n132\t|-----|----------|-------|\n133\t| HLR-1 — serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto | P0 | E005 (KServe + serving data plane), E004 (Platform Operator); enabled by onboarding chain E001, E002, E003 |\n134\t| HLR-2 — compliance evidence package assembled and submitted through security review board to external lab | P0 | E007 (attestations sourced from E003's pipeline, inventory and test results from E004/E005) |\n135\t| HLR-3 — CI enforces FIPS regressions via gating check | P1 | E006 |\n136\t| HLR-4 — documentation of FIPS support posture and prerequisites | P2 | E008 |\n137\t\n138\tAll four HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements; no HLR split across\n139\tredundant epics.\n140\t\n141\t## Health Warnings\n142\t\n143\t1. **Platform Operator not in architecture context** (non-blocking, verify):\n144\t   `.context/architecture-context/` contains only the KServe file. Per the\n145\t   operational platform-inclusion check the Platform Operator is treated as\n146\t   not-yet-onboarded and an onboarding chain (E001–E003) was generated. If\n147\t   the operator already exists in the platform (i.e., the context gap is a\n148\t   data gap, not a real gap), E001–E003 should be dropped and E004 re-based\n149\t   onto the existing repo/pipeline. In that case E007's scope still spans the\n150\t   whole serving stack including the Platform Operator images (module\n151\t   inventory and test results) — that span is part of E007's evidence-package\n152\t   scope and must remain visible regardless of where the operator\n153\t   implementation is based.\n154\t2. **Team attribution assumed** (non-blocking): the strategy contains no team\n155\t   data. RHOAI Serving comes from the KServe architecture context; \"RHOAI\n156\t   Platform\" for the Platform Operator epics is an assumption (no\n157\t   architecture context available); the docs epic is assigned to RHOAI\n158\t   Serving as subject-matter owner.\n159\t3. **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams, Dependencies table,\n160\t   Acceptance Criteria, and NFRs are absent; acceptance criteria were derived\n161\t   from HLRs and the problem statement.\n162\t4. **External schedule constraints are not ACs**: lab turnaround (historically\n163\t   six to nine months) and review-board cadence are externally controlled.\n164\t   They are recorded as schedule risk on E007; E007's ACs are written at the\n165\t   engineering completion point (package assembled, review-board sign-off\n166\t   recorded, package submitted to the lab and triaged).\n167\t5. **Docs-epic priority**: HLR-4 is P2, but per the `docs-authoring` priority\n168\t   exception the docs epic derives P0 from the strategy's Critical priority.\n169\t   This does not push priority upstream (its P0 prerequisites are already P0).\n170\t6. **CI gate enablement sequencing**: E006 is parallel to E005 by DAG rules\n171\t   (independent work products). Operationally the gate should be enabled once\n172\t   the remediated baseline exists — captured as an AC on E006, and as a\n173\t   coordination note here.\n174\t7. **Serving data plane span**: the problem statement includes the serving\n175\t   data plane (runtime images) in the crypto remediation, while Affected\n176\t   Components lists only KServe and Platform Operator. The remediation epic\n177\t   covers serving-stack images including the data plane under canonical\n178\t   component `KServe`; if data-plane runtime remediation turns out to need\n179\t   separate repos/review cycles, it may warrant its own epic under\n180\t   `Serving Runtimes`.\n181\t\n182\t## Tiered Delivery\n183\t\n184\tThe external certification lab has uncertain, externally controlled timing,\n185\tbut the strategy delivers value without the certification outcome:\n186\t\n187\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001–E006 — FIPS-correct serving-stack\n188\t  images running on FIPS-enabled clusters, CI regression protection, and the\n189\t  onboarded operator repo/pipeline. None of this is gated by the lab.\n190\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: E007 — evidence-package submission and the\n191\t  certification outcome. The submission milestone is engineered (package\n192\t  assembled, review-board sign-off, submitted and triaged); the lab's\n193\t  certification decision and turnaround are not compressible by engineering\n194\t  effort.\n195\t\n196\tNo upstream PR/RFC epic is needed (remediation is carried midstream), so no\n197\texternal-dependency Implementation epic is generated.\n198\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1b4f8e1156e58df","agent_id":"ae94751e2b2cffef3","session_id":"4a043728-3f47-48a2-8b4d-164c061f2c18","uuid":"7e1bd25a-5e9d-446f-b00c-69b5aae2a77d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T01:39:05.219Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bed413926210da9a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
