. all present ✓.\n\n    Hmm, one more check on Rule 11 and the validator: validator checks \"docs-last coverage\" — passed ✓.\n\n    Also \"priority inheritance (lower-priority epic blocking P0 work)\" — E006 (P1) blocks E008 (P0 docs). Docs-authoring exempt ✓. Also, does E006 block any other P0? No. ✓.\n\n    Now Criterion 1 more carefully — \"Check for priority collapse — if an epic maps to HLRs at multiple priority levels and the lower-priority HLRs are distinct deferrable features\". E008 maps only to HLR-4. E005 maps HLR-1 primarily (module inventory feeds HLR-2 — but that's an input artifact, not a separate feature). E006 maps HLR-3 only. No collapse. ✓ 2/2.\n\n    Criterion 3: any oversized epic? E005: audit + remediate KServe components AND serving data plane images, verify both deployment modes. Hmm — the data plane images (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit runtimes per architecture context — Serving Runtimes component). E005 bundles KServe component images + Serving Runtimes data plane images into one epic under component KServe. Health Warning 7 addresses this: if data-plane runtime remediation needs separate repos/review cycles, it may warrant its own epic under Serving Runtimes. Is this a boundary violation (epic spans multiple components)? The strategy's problem statement treats \"KServe components and the serving data plane\" as one remediation scope; Affected Components lists only KServe and Platform Operator. The remediation is the same logical change (swap non-validated crypto) across serving-stack images, owned by RHOAI Serving. The \"unless same logical change\" carve-out applies. And the health warning flags the risk. I'd note it as acceptable — maybe worth a minor? The rubric says \"No single epic spans multiple components or teams (unless same logical change)\" — same logical change carve-out applies. Not an issue. Possibly worth acknowledging in the criterion text.\n\n    Criterion 4: Type correctness. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts